# KNOWN GAP (todo/t0957) — an indexed callee with a VARIABLE index is rejected,
# while the same program with a literal index compiles and runs.
#
#   fs[0](21)      -> 42        (literal index: the speculative type parse
#                                fails on the `0`, the parser backtracks, the
#                                EIndex survives)
#   fs[n + 0](21)  -> 42        (the `+` breaks the type parse the same way)
#   fs[n](21)      -> rc 1      error[E_NotAFunction]: 'fs' is not a function
#
# A bare identifier inside the brackets parses fine as a type NAME, so the
# generic-call reading `fs[n](21)` succeeds and the index read is DELETED from
# the AST before the typechecker ever sees it. The diagnostic is honest about
# the AST it was handed; the AST is wrong.
#
# The two settled spellings are pinned by `callable_callee_shape_axis.gg`
# (cells 3 and 5); this one is here because the disambiguation rule for
# `expr[ident](args)` is not ratified — see todo/t0957 for the two candidate
# rules.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the #[ignore]d wired test): prints `42`.

void main():
    Vector[Callable[int(int)]] fs = []
    fs.push((int x): x * 2)
    int n = 0
    print(f"{fs[n](21)}")
